WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.048
Comparing collider measurements with theory
requires unfolding, that is,

00:00:05.128 --> 00:00:09.289
correcting the distortions that detectors introduce
into the data.

00:00:09.369 --> 00:00:13.274
But most unfolding methods first bin the data into
histograms,

00:00:13.354 --> 00:00:17.837
while many theory predictions live at the level of
statistical moments.

00:00:17.917 --> 00:00:20.344
This paper introduces Moment Unfolding,

00:00:20.424 --> 00:00:26.128
a machine-learning method that directly unfolds
distribution moments without ever binning.

00:00:26.208 --> 00:00:32.363
Inspired by Generative Adversarial Networks and by
Boltzmann's approach to statistical mechanics,

00:00:32.443 --> 00:00:38.340
it recovers moments to sub-percent accuracy on both
Gaussian toy data and simulated LHC jets.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:41.044
Unfolding, also known as deconvolution,

00:00:41.124 --> 00:00:46.282
corrects the distortions a detector imprints on
measured data so that experiments can

00:00:46.362 --> 00:00:48.994
be compared with each other and with theory.

00:00:49.074 --> 00:00:54.479
The usual recipe unfolds an entire spectrum after
first discretizing it into a histogram,

00:00:54.559 --> 00:00:57.067
then computes moments from that histogram.

00:00:57.147 --> 00:01:00.642
But this binning step introduces discretization artifacts,

00:01:00.722 --> 00:01:06.250
and it is wasteful when the quantity you actually
care about is just a small set of moments

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:08.469
as a function of another observable.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:14.570
That mismatch between binned data and moment-level
theory predictions is the gap this paper closes.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:18.723
Summarizing a distribution with a few moments makes
it tractable to visualize and,

00:01:18.803 --> 00:01:22.577
crucially, to predict from first principles.

00:01:22.657 --> 00:01:26.430
For example,

00:01:26.510 --> 00:01:30.283
the full densities of hadronic jets cannot be
computed in perturbative QCD,

00:01:30.363 --> 00:01:34.137
but the energy dependence of their moments can be.

00:01:34.217 --> 00:01:37.990
Unbinned unfolding methods already exist and avoid
binning artifacts,

00:01:38.070 --> 00:01:41.843
but they are built to unfold entire spectra,

00:01:41.923 --> 00:01:45.697
so they may trade away precision on the handful of
moments a physicist actually wants.

00:01:45.777 --> 00:01:49.550
This motivates a dedicated method that unfolds the
moments themselves.

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:52.547
The core contribution is Moment Unfolding,

00:01:52.627 --> 00:01:56.078
a new unbinned and non-iterative reweighting technique.

00:01:56.158 --> 00:02:00.573
It learns a reweighting function, playing the role
of a GAN generator,

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:06.094
whose form is inspired by the Boltzmann factor so
that its trainable parameters can be

00:02:06.174 --> 00:02:09.305
directly identified with the observable's moments.

00:02:09.385 --> 00:02:14.056
A discriminator pushes the reweighted simulation to
match the target data.

00:02:14.136 --> 00:02:18.808
Unlike OmniFold, which trains a fresh pair of
networks on every iteration,

00:02:18.888 --> 00:02:22.660
Moment Unfolding trains a single pair of networks
just once.

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.278
The method borrows Boltzmann's idea of building the
distribution that maximizes entropy subject to fixed moments.

00:02:27.358 --> 00:02:31.596
Concretely,

00:02:31.676 --> 00:02:35.914
the generator is written as the exponential of a
polynomial in the observable,

00:02:35.994 --> 00:02:40.232
so its coefficients, the lambdas, are the moments
being unfolded.

00:02:40.312 --> 00:02:44.550
This generator reweights the simulated events,

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:48.868
and a discriminator neural network tries to tell
the reweighted simulation apart from the real data.

00:02:48.948 --> 00:02:53.186
The two are trained against each other on a
weighted binary cross-entropy loss: the discriminator

00:02:53.266 --> 00:02:57.504
minimizes it while the generator maximizes it.

00:02:57.584 --> 00:03:01.822
Because the reweighting only changes importance
weights and not the event features,

00:03:01.902 --> 00:03:06.140
the expensive detector emulation runs a single time.

00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:08.856
The method is tested on two problems.

00:03:08.936 --> 00:03:12.382
First, a Gaussian toy: the truth is a standard normal,

00:03:12.462 --> 00:03:15.582
the generation is shifted to mean minus one-half,

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:18.324
and the detector adds wide Gaussian noise,

00:03:18.404 --> 00:03:22.699
with a million samples split three to one for
training and testing.

00:03:22.779 --> 00:03:25.964
Because a Gaussian has only finitely many moments,

00:03:26.044 --> 00:03:30.339
unfolding its moments is equivalent to unfolding
the whole density.

00:03:30.419 --> 00:03:35.824
Second, hadronic jets from simulated LHC
collisions, using the jet width observable,

00:03:35.904 --> 00:03:41.505
drawn from the same Pythia and Herwig plus Delphes
datasets used in the OmniFold paper,

00:03:41.585 --> 00:03:46.860
where one simulation stands in for data and the
other for the synthetic reference.

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:49.522
The results are strong on both tasks.

00:03:49.602 --> 00:03:54.311
For the Gaussian example, the loss function peaks
exactly at the true mean,

00:03:54.391 --> 00:03:57.375
confirming the method recovers the right answer,

00:03:57.455 --> 00:04:00.950
and the discriminator converges within about ten epochs.

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:06.058
For the jet width, the team unfolds the first and
second moments simultaneously.

00:04:06.138 --> 00:04:13.017
Scanning the loss as a function of each candidate
moment produces curves whose peaks land on the true values,

00:04:13.097 --> 00:04:17.359
with a mean absolute error of two hundredths of a
percent or better.

00:04:17.439 --> 00:04:21.700
That is sub-percent agreement between the unfolded
and true moments.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:27.334
One instructive observation concerns the limits of
unfolding only a couple of moments.

00:04:27.414 --> 00:04:32.110
After Moment Unfolding matches the first and second
moments of the jet width,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:38.064
the full distributions of truth and reweighted
generation still are not statistically identical.

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:43.399
The reason is simply that higher moments remain
relevant and were not part of the fit.

00:04:43.479 --> 00:04:45.011
This is expected behavior,

00:04:45.091 --> 00:04:50.655
and it clarifies that the technique deliberately
controls the specific moments you ask for,

00:04:50.735 --> 00:04:52.020
leaving the rest free.

00:04:52.400 --> 00:04:54.632
A few numbers capture the impact.

00:04:54.712 --> 00:05:02.130
The unfolded moments agree with the true moments to
within two hundredths of a percent mean absolute error.

00:05:02.210 --> 00:05:05.634
Two moments of the jet width are unfolded at once.

00:05:05.714 --> 00:05:08.857
The discriminator converges within ten epochs.

00:05:08.937 --> 00:05:14.393
The Gaussian study uses a million samples with a
three-to-one train-test split,

00:05:14.473 --> 00:05:21.120
and the entire set of notebooks reproduces in under
five minutes on a single Nvidia RTX6000 GPU.

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:26.824
The takeaway is that you can unfold detector
effects directly at the level of moments,

00:05:26.904 --> 00:05:28.709
without ever binning the data.

00:05:28.789 --> 00:05:34.114
Moment Unfolding does this with a GAN-like
generator whose parameters are the moments,

00:05:34.194 --> 00:05:36.564
trains only once rather than iterating,

00:05:36.644 --> 00:05:43.037
and recovers the true moments to better than a
hundredth of a percent on realistic LHC jet simulations.

00:05:43.117 --> 00:05:46.116
Because the algorithm is agnostic to the dataset,

00:05:46.196 --> 00:05:51.520
the same idea could carry over to deconvolution
problems well beyond particle physics.
